<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest>
  <!--
    The version here is what the Power Platform upgrades on, and the release
    workflow refuses to publish a tag that disagrees with it. Bump this,
    Solution/src/Other/Solution.xml and package.json in the same commit.
  -->
  <control namespace="PCFHub"
           constructor="AttachmentList"
           version="0.1.0"
           display-name-key="AttachmentList_Name"
           description-key="AttachmentList_Desc"
           control-type="standard">

    <external-service-usage enabled="false" />

    <!--
      PROPERTY-SET ROLES, AND THE REASON IS NOT THE ONE THE TEMPLATE GIVES.

      The template's rule — declare roles when the control assigns meaning to
      specific columns — is satisfied here obviously enough: a file name is not
      "a column", it is what `FileObject.fileName` has to be, and the size and
      the MIME type are two more fields of that same object. But the argument
      that settles it is narrower.

      **The out-of-the-box Notes associated view carries subject, notetext,
      createdon and modifiedby. It does not carry filesize, mimetype or
      isdocument.** A control that declared no roles and matched hardcoded
      logical names off `dataset.columns` would therefore find no size, no MIME
      type and no way to tell a file from a plain note on the single most common
      configuration this control will ever be dropped onto — and would fail
      *silently*, handing `openFile` an empty mimeType and putting a download
      button on rows with nothing to download.

      A mapped role puts the column into the query, and an unmapped one is
      visible to the maker at configuration time rather than to the user at
      click time.

      They also buy something a hardcoded read could not: the download reads
      `dataset.getTargetEntityType()` rather than the string "annotation", so a
      custom attachment table of the same shape works unchanged.

      ONLY `fileNameColumn` IS REQUIRED. Six mandatory mappings is real friction
      on a control whose pitch is "point it at the Notes subgrid", so every
      other role degrades to a stated fallback and the display names say which
      annotation column each one expects.

      **THE FIXTURE RULE.** A role is *found* on the column by `alias` and the
      value is *read* off the record by `name`. Backwards, the control renders
      nothing against a real view while looking perfect against a fixture that
      sets the two to the same string — which is how that bug reached production
      in pcf-tag-list. `dev/fixture.js` and `demo/records.json` set `alias` to
      the role name and `name` to the annotation logical name, so a mistake
      fails where it can be seen.

      `cds-data-set-options` keeps the subgrid's own chrome. The command bar is
      on because this control adds and deletes nothing: New and Delete are the
      platform's, above a list that only reads and downloads. Quick find is on
      because a record with forty attachments is common and this control has no
      search of its own.
    -->
    <data-set name="records"
              display-name-key="Records_DataSet_Name"
              cds-data-set-options="displayCommandBar:true;displayQuickFind:true">
      <property-set name="fileNameColumn"
                    display-name-key="fileNameColumn_Name"
                    description-key="fileNameColumn_Desc"
                    of-type="SingleLine.Text"
                    usage="bound"
                    required="true" />
      <property-set name="fileSizeColumn"
                    display-name-key="fileSizeColumn_Name"
                    description-key="fileSizeColumn_Desc"
                    of-type="Whole.None"
                    usage="bound"
                    required="false" />
      <property-set name="mimeTypeColumn"
                    display-name-key="mimeTypeColumn_Name"
                    description-key="mimeTypeColumn_Desc"
                    of-type="SingleLine.Text"
                    usage="bound"
                    required="false" />
      <property-set name="isDocumentColumn"
                    display-name-key="isDocumentColumn_Name"
                    description-key="isDocumentColumn_Desc"
                    of-type="TwoOptions"
                    usage="bound"
                    required="false" />
      <property-set name="subjectColumn"
                    display-name-key="subjectColumn_Name"
                    description-key="subjectColumn_Desc"
                    of-type="SingleLine.Text"
                    usage="bound"
                    required="false" />
      <property-set name="createdOnColumn"
                    display-name-key="createdOnColumn_Name"
                    description-key="createdOnColumn_Desc"
                    of-type="DateAndTime.DateAndTime"
                    usage="bound"
                    required="false" />
    </data-set>

    <!--
      The column the file's bytes live in, as a logical name.

      Not a role, because it is deliberately NOT on the view: `documentbody` is
      a base64 blob and a view that selected it would download every attachment
      on the record every time the form opened. It is fetched one row at a time,
      on the press, through `webAPI.retrieveRecord`.

      It is interpolated into a query string, so index.ts validates it against
      /^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$/ before use and falls back to the default. A merely
      *wrong* logical name is let through, so the server's own message names it
      rather than this control guessing.
    -->
    <property name="bodyColumn"
              display-name-key="bodyColumn_Name"
              description-key="bodyColumn_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="documentbody" />

    <!--
      Defaults off, so the list shows what the subgrid shows.

      A `default-value="false"` reaches PCFHub's demo harness as the raw XML
      *string* "false", and `Boolean("false")` is `true` — so this property
      arrives switched ON on the one surface the public sees unless it is read
      through a normaliser. index.ts has one; every preset sets it explicitly.
    -->
    <property name="hideTextNotes"
              display-name-key="hideTextNotes_Name"
              description-key="hideTextNotes_Desc"
              of-type="TwoOptions"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="false" />

    <!--
      The ceiling, checked BEFORE the retrieve rather than after.

      `retrieveRecord` has no streaming and no abort, so a 200 MB attachment
      arrives as one base64 string in memory and there is no refusing it once it
      is there. Refusing on the row's own declared size is the only refusal
      available.
    -->
    <property name="maxDownloadSizeMb"
              display-name-key="maxDownloadSizeMb_Name"
              description-key="maxDownloadSizeMb_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="32" />

    <property name="pageSize"
              display-name-key="pageSize_Name"
              description-key="pageSize_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="25" />

    <!--
      Set before the download is attempted, and set even on a host that cannot
      download at all.

      That is the point of it: `navigation.openFile` is documented model-driven
      apps only, so in a canvas app this output is the *only* route a maker has.
      They bind it, handle OnChange, and do the download themselves. See
      docs/canvas.md.
    -->
    <property name="downloadedRecordId"
              display-name-key="downloadedRecordId_Name"
              description-key="downloadedRecordId_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="output" />

    <resources>
      <code path="index.ts" order="1" />
      <css path="css/AttachmentList.css" order="1" />
      <!--
        THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE AND 1033 GOES LAST. `pcf-start` picks the .resx
        by manifest order rather than by locale — it reads whichever file is
        listed last and ignores the LCID entirely. The platform itself ignores
        the order completely, so `npm start` is the only thing in the world that
        reads it. ADD A NEW LANGUAGE ABOVE THIS LINE.
      -->
      <resx path="strings/AttachmentList.3082.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/AttachmentList.1036.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/AttachmentList.1031.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/AttachmentList.1041.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/AttachmentList.1033.resx" version="1.0.0" />
    </resources>

    <!--
      WEBAPI, AND `required="false"` IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A LIST THAT
      DEGRADES AND ONE THAT WILL NOT START.

      The documented behaviour of `required="true"` on a host that does not
      support a feature is not a null accessor to check — it is a design-time
      warning and *component load failure at runtime*. WebAPI is Dataverse-
      dependent and is not available in canvas apps, so `required="true"` would
      mean a control that renders nothing at all there rather than one that
      lists the notes and declines to download.

      Listing is most of what this control does. With `required="false"` the
      host leaves `context.webAPI` absent instead of refusing to load, index.ts
      feature-detects it, and canvas gets a list plus an output property.

      One feature, and it is earned: the file's bytes are not on the view and
      there is no other way to reach them. `navigation.openFile` needs no
      feature — it is a method on context.navigation, which is not gated.
    -->
    <feature-usage>
      <uses-feature name="WebAPI" required="false" />
    </feature-usage>
  </control>
</manifest>
